## AI Agent Reasoning

### Context
IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md item 1 (highest priority): spec v3 §2.3 required
drag-across-cells range marking, Shift+Click bulk range selection, and a
real multi-step undo/redo stack (Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Shift+Z), on top of the
already-existing but frontend-unused POST /api/entries/bulk endpoint.

### Decisions Made
- Kept HabitCell's existing onToggle(date)/onArrow(date,key) single-arg
  contracts unchanged and added new *optional* props (onShiftToggle,
  onShiftArrow, onCellMouseDown/onCellMouseEnter) instead of changing
  onToggle's signature to include shiftKey. This avoided breaking the
  pre-existing HabitCell.test.tsx assertion
  `expect(onToggle).toHaveBeenCalledWith('2026-07-10')` (a 2-arg call
  would have failed that assertion) and kept the diff additive.
- Drag commit only fires onRangeToggle if the pointer moved to a
  different column between mousedown and mouseup. Real browsers don't
  fire a click event when mousedown/mouseup happen on different
  elements, but a plain click (no movement) does both a mousedown+mouseup
  AND a click on the same element — without this guard, a simple click
  would apply the toggle twice (once via the range path, once via
  onToggle), silently reverting it. Verified via a dedicated test
  ('does not fire onRangeToggle for a mousedown/mouseup on the same
  cell').
- Range target value convention: for pointer-drag, the anchor cell's
  *current* marked state determines the target for the whole range
  (targetDone = !isMarked(anchorCell)) — mirrors 'click toggles this
  cell, drag extends that same toggle to the whole range'. Shift+Click
  and Shift+Arrow use the same convention against the clicked/target
  cell so behavior is consistent across all three interaction modes.
- Shift+Click/Shift+Arrow share one ref-based 'range session'
  (shiftAnchor) so repeated Shift+ArrowRight presses keep extending the
  same range (spreadsheet-like) instead of restarting from the last
  cursor position each keypress. Any plain (non-shift) interaction
  clears the session.
- Undo/redo model: switched from a single flat undo array of
  (habitId,date) pairs to undoStack/redoStack of Action = Change[]
  (Change = {habitId, date, prevDone, nextDone}), so a whole drag/Shift
  range undoes or redoes as one atomic action via a single
  bulkSetEntries call, not N individual toggles. Any new action clears
  the redo stack (standard undo/redo semantics).
- Bug found and fixed during implementation: committing the range
  inside setDragState's functional updater (setDragState(prev => {...
  side effect ...})) triggered a real React
  'Cannot update a component while rendering a different component'
  warning, because bulkSetEntries synchronously calls setTick() before
  its first await. Fixed by mirroring drag state into a plain
  dragStateRef and doing the commit as an ordinary function call from
  the native window 'mouseup' listener, outside any setState updater.

### Trade-offs
- bulkSetEntries persists undo/redo 'unmark' actions as entries with
  done=false (via the bulk endpoint) rather than DELETEing them like
  the original single-cell toggle() does. This differs slightly from
  toggle()'s DB row lifecycle but is behaviorally identical for the UI
  (only done=true entries are ever rendered as marked), and reuses the
  same bulk endpoint uniformly for range apply, undo, and redo.
- No new Playwright e2e coverage was added for drag/Shift-range/undo-
  redo in this pass; relied on unit test coverage (HabitTable.test.tsx,
  HabitApp.test.tsx) as the primary signal given time constraints. Left
  as a follow-up in IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md if deeper confidence is
  needed on real browser drag behavior.

